#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161735 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "42777bd2-44d0-5117-9e8b-ee722d8f3229" :question/math-academy-id "e-8276" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2 = 20$." :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 20\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\nBy solving the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\mid A-\\lambda I \\mid = 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=20 \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\lambda_2=10\n$$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=20$, we have\n$A - 20I|= \\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix} - 20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{10}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=10$, we have\n$A - 10I|= \\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix} - 10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{10}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161736 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "3564c452-1a51-5455-a6b1-223fda1611c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8277" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2= 70$?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2\n$$\n\nTo determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}6 - λ & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 3λ - 70 &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 7,-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2$.\n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7y_{1}^{2} - 10y_{2}^{2} &= 70 \\\\\n\\frac{y_{1}^{2}}{10} - \\frac{y_{2}^{2}}{7} &= 1 \\\\\n(y_{1}^{2})/((\\sqrt{10})^{2}) - (y_{2}^{2})/((\\sqrt{7})^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161750 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "ec194ea7-df30-5370-b0c4-d27d4b34f8c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8473" :question/problem "Draw a diagram that depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/fa1f2ef5edfdc2471cf95a125c9702a1.png)" :question/worked-solution "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation.\n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/2503cf232548072ca23d6152156ff107.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161773 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "ba46e82f-10b0-529c-94b0-3aebb7d1e1a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8678" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1$. Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=1$, find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A$. Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3$, we have\n$A - 3I|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=1$, we have\n$A - 1I|= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} - 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82df2d8-739f-528c-9659-e7455314d874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161041} {:answer/id #uuid "dd714eb1-6ea2-5d75-a835-c733ffb76b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161042} {:answer/id #uuid "17a9b86b-069e-5b49-9c8c-32881eafb218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161043} {:answer/id #uuid "35a98124-f296-5b5a-8584-d560183140f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161044} {:answer/id #uuid "6366b245-182f-5919-bd2d-fc4f9a968707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3\\sqrt{11}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b82df2d8-739f-528c-9659-e7455314d874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161041} :answer-field/id #uuid "f74724ff-3a70-562a-91c3-99f5b0955233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161040}] :question/id #uuid "151c3599-c63a-5e6a-8614-ca5bcf398293" :question/math-academy-id "q-94596" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $2x_{1}^{2} - 6x_{1}x_{2} + 10x_{2}^{2} = 44$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a23584f-4232-5a34-9cf2-8913689e8fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/q-96031-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161275} {:answer/id #uuid "c36067ad-b8f6-5114-a6d9-1910f202198a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/q-96031-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161276} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1099bd-4821-5c2f-82a6-2deb7dadf175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/q-96031-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161277} {:answer/id #uuid "41b701f8-2014-59dc-9964-39560ba34974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/q-96031-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161278} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9d8cf7-5115-59a6-b4ce-8a99d83728b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/q-96031-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41b701f8-2014-59dc-9964-39560ba34974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/q-96031-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161278} :answer-field/id #uuid "a974994b-7115-58ea-a231-959690e5e5fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161274}] :question/id #